// Formative practice quiz for loading-and-error-states. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/loading-and-error-states

::loading-and-error-states o1 - one status::A component tracks isLoading, hasError and data in separate state variables, and can end up showing a spinner and an error at the same time. What is the better model?{
=One status value with loading, success and error as its possible values#Correct. A single status makes overlapping states impossible.
~A fourth boolean to track the overlap#Not quite. More flags allow more impossible combinations.
~A global variable outside React#Not quite. That does not change the states, and it stops React from rendering.
~Rendering all three at once and hiding them with CSS#Not quite. That still keeps conflicting states on the page.
}

::loading-and-error-states o2 - response.ok::Why check response.ok after fetch?{
~fetch rejects for a 404, and ok tells you why#Not quite. fetch does not reject for a 404. It only rejects when the request itself fails.
~It turns the body into JSON#Not quite. response.json does that. response.ok only reports the status range.
~It retries failed requests#Not quite. response.ok is a read-only boolean. It does not retry anything.
=fetch resolves for statuses such as 404, so you must detect them yourself#Correct. A completed response resolves the promise whatever its status code.
}

::loading-and-error-states o2 - network failure::The server is unreachable. Where does a fetch chain end up?{
~response.ok is false#Not quite. No response exists, so there is no ok property to read.
=The promise rejects, so the catch runs#Correct. A network failure is a real rejection, unlike a 404 response.
~response.json returns an error object#Not quite. There is no response to parse.
~fetch waits forever and never settles#Not quite. A failed network request rejects.
}

::loading-and-error-states o2 - abort handling::A cleanup aborts a request when a newer one starts. How should the old request be treated?{
~Show the error screen#Not quite. That would flash an error while the newer request is running fine.
~Retry the aborted request#Not quite. The point of aborting is that it is no longer wanted.
=Ignore the AbortError, since you cancelled it on purpose#Correct. An abort is not a failure the user needs to hear about.
~Reload the page#Not quite. Nothing has gone wrong, and a reload would lose state.
}

::loading-and-error-states o3 - empty vs error::A request returns 200 with an empty list. What should the screen show?{
~An error message#Not quite. That tells users something broke when the request worked.
~A loading spinner#Not quite. Loading has finished, so a spinner misleads.
=A clear empty message such as\: no results#Correct. Nothing failed, and the screen should say there is simply nothing to show.
~Nothing at all#Not quite. A blank area looks like a bug and gives no information.
}
